============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_024

## Description

A container deployment platform is used to manage production deployments across multiple Kubernetes clusters. Development teams build container images through Jenkins pipelines, store them in an internal artifact registry, and deploy them through a centralized deployment service.

To protect the software supply chain, the organization requires that only cryptographically signed container images may be deployed. The build pipeline generates image digests, requests signatures from a signing service, and stores signature records alongside artifact metadata. Kubernetes admission control is configured to validate image signatures before allowing deployments.

The deployment workflow spans multiple components. Jenkinsfile builds and publishes images. BuildService.cs registers artifact metadata and image digests. SignatureService.java generates signatures for image digests and stores them through SignatureRepository.java. DeploymentService.cs creates deployment requests which are later evaluated by Kubernetes admission control. AdmissionWebhook.java invokes ImagePolicyValidator.java to determine whether a deployment should be permitted.

At first glance, the platform appears to implement strong supply-chain security controls. Signature validation exists, trusted signers are enforced, admission control blocks unsigned artifacts, and deployment requests require associated signature records.

The vulnerability exists because artifact authenticity is inferred from the existence of a valid signature rather than verification that the signature belongs to the artifact being deployed. Signature validation verifies that a signature exists and originates from a trusted signing key, but never verifies that the deployed artifact digest matches the digest originally signed by the signing service.

As a result, an attacker can deploy a malicious artifact while presenting a valid signature generated for a different artifact. Since admission control validates signer trust but never validates digest-to-signature binding, the malicious artifact inherits trust from an unrelated signed artifact.

The vulnerability is not caused by Jenkins, Kubernetes configuration, deployment approvals, registry authentication, or artifact storage. The issue occurs because the deployment validation workflow fails to enforce the security invariant that a valid signature must correspond to the exact artifact digest being deployed.


