============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_027

## Description

An electronic health record (EHR) platform allows hospitals and healthcare providers to assign external specialists to patient care teams. Specialists may be granted temporary access to patient records while participating in active treatment, consultations, emergency reviews, or specialist evaluations.

The platform maintains assignment information in an authoritative assignment repository. Every authorization decision is intended to be scoped to a specific specialist-patient relationship. A specialist should only be able to access records belonging to patients for whom an active assignment exists.

To reduce authorization latency, the platform maintains a Redis-backed authorization cache. Assignment changes trigger cache refresh operations, and authorization decisions are subsequently served from cached access records instead of repeatedly querying the assignment repository.

The authoritative assignment system correctly models access using both specialist identifiers and patient identifiers. Assignment records, access grants, and authorization scope helpers consistently define access as a specialist-patient relationship.

However, during cache key generation, authorization state is transformed into specialist-scoped cache entries rather than patient-scoped cache entries. The cache layer stores authorization records using keys derived only from specialist identity. As specialists are reassigned between patients, multiple authorization scopes collapse into the same cache entry.

The cache refresh workflow operates correctly and refreshes authorization state whenever assignments change. However, because cache keys do not preserve patient context, refreshed authorization records overwrite one another and continue representing stale authorization state.

The record access gateway subsequently trusts cached authorization decisions when retrieving patient records. Because authorization state has been collapsed into specialist-level cache entries, a specialist may retain access to patient records after assignment revocation if another active assignment refreshes the same cache key.

The vulnerability is not caused by cache invalidation failures, missing refresh operations, excessive cache lifetimes, authorization policy misconfiguration, or repository errors. The root cause is the loss of authorization scope while transforming assignment data into cached authorization decisions.


