============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_093

## Description

A cloud-native scientific compute orchestration platform lets research organizations submit large-scale experiments to a shared distributed compute cluster. Before any computation runs, every experiment passes through a multi-stage pipeline: an admission stage checks whether the request is authorized, a packaging stage turns the authorized request into concrete units of work, a routing stage moves those units toward available compute capacity, an execution stage collects the results those units produce, and a publishing stage assembles the collected results into the experiment's final output.

Authorization for an experiment run is not a single flag — it is a combination of which organization submitted it, which experiment it belongs to, which specific execution permit was issued for this run, and which compute generation that permit corresponds to. All four pieces together are what make one execution run distinguishable from any other run of the same experiment, including runs that have since been revoked, superseded, or re-authorized.

The admission stage does this correctly: it confirms the submitting organization is active and owns the experiment, confirms the experiment is in a state that's eligible to run, confirms the organization has quota available, and confirms the execution permit presented is valid, unexpired, and matches both the organization and the experiment. Only after all of that succeeds does it produce a single authoritative record containing the complete four-part identity for the run.

As that record moves forward through packaging, routing, and execution, each stage only needs a subset of the original identity to do its own job — a packaging stage needs to know what to compute, a routing stage needs to know where to send it, an execution stage needs to know when results from the same run should be grouped together. Several of these stages, in deciding what they don't need to carry forward, each independently narrow the identity a little further. No single stage's decision is unreasonable on its own, and each one leaves enough identity behind to keep functioning normally.

By the time results reach consolidation, though, the identity that survives is no longer the full four-part identity admission produced — it has narrowed down to just the organization and the experiment, the two facts that never change across the entire lifetime of an experiment regardless of how many times it has been run, revoked, or re-authorized. Grouping results by only those two facts means that output produced under a run whose permit has since been revoked, or whose compute generation has since been superseded, is indistinguishable from output produced under the run that is currently authorized. Both are simply "results for this organization's experiment."

The consequence is that historical or no-longer-authorized computation can be merged into, and published as part of, the currently active execution of an experiment — without any single stage having made an obviously wrong decision.


