Application ID: scenario_046

## Description

An enterprise machine learning platform manages the complete lifecycle of model training, artifact registration, packaging, and deployment. Every trained model is registered with an authoritative artifact manifest describing the dataset, training code, dependency graph, and cryptographic signature used to produce the model. Independently, the deployment subsystem constructs and caches deployment packages containing the runtime environment required to execute the model.

Before deployment, the platform combines the artifact manifest retrieved from the model registry with a deployment package obtained from the deployment subsystem. The deployment service assumes both objects describe the same model provenance and performs extensive validation before releasing the model into production.

The attack requires the interaction of **two independent vulnerabilities located in separate architectural branches**. First, during construction of the operational artifact manifest, the model's `dependencyDigest` is silently omitted even though it is preserved and validated throughout the projection pipeline. Second, the deployment cache identifies deployment packages using only the model identifier rather than both the model identifier and dependency digest. Individually, neither weakness is sufficient to produce an exploit. Together they allow deployment packages generated from older dependency graphs to be combined with newer artifact manifests, breaking provenance continuity across the deployment workflow.

As a result, the deployment platform may release models using stale runtime dependencies even though every individual component successfully validates the operational objects it receives.

