============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_022

## Description

A digital evidence management platform allows investigators to upload compressed evidence archives for indexing and review. Uploaded archives are validated, extracted, indexed, and made available through an evidence browser used during investigations and legal discovery workflows.

The platform consists of multiple processing stages implemented across different technologies. A Go-based API accepts evidence uploads and performs archive validation. A Rust extraction service unpacks archive contents into isolated extraction directories. A Python indexing pipeline recursively discovers extracted files, collects metadata, and stores file references for later retrieval. Evidence files can then be downloaded through the evidence browser.

Several security controls appear to protect the archive processing workflow. ArchiveValidationService.go blocks directory traversal sequences, rejects absolute paths, validates archive sizes, and restricts allowed file types. ExtractionPolicy.rs enforces extraction boundaries and validates archive entry paths before extraction occurs.

The vulnerability emerges through symbolic link handling. ArchiveExtractor.rs intentionally preserves symbolic links contained within uploaded archives. SymlinkHandler.rs creates extracted symbolic links without validating the destination target. The extraction layer assumes that later processing stages will safely handle extracted filesystem objects.

The indexing layer violates this assumption. EvidenceIndexer.py recursively indexes extracted content and FileLocator.py resolves indexed paths using realpath() without verifying that resolved filesystem locations remain inside the extraction boundary. As a result, symbolic links contained within an uploaded archive can reference files or directories outside the extraction root.

An attacker can upload a crafted archive containing symbolic links to sensitive filesystem locations. During indexing, the platform treats the referenced external files as legitimate evidence artifacts. These files become indexed, searchable, and downloadable through the evidence management system.


