============================================================
APPLICATION DESCRIPTION
============================================================

Application ID: scenario_085

## Description

Large enterprises execute tenant workloads inside isolated runtime environments on a shared compute fabric. A workload cannot start until three independent infrastructure controllers have each finished preparing their own runtime resource: a Volume Controller provisions storage, a Network Controller provisions network access, and an Execution Profile Controller provisions the sandbox, tool allowlist, and CPU policy the workload will run under. Each controller owns exactly one trust domain and none of them communicate with each other directly. Instead, each publishes an independent runtime descriptor once its own provisioning step succeeds.

A composition service reads all three descriptors and combines them into a single immutable runtime record. That record is what the compute orchestrator uses to decide whether a workload may be scheduled, and what the container launch service uses to actually configure the isolation boundary the workload runs inside. In the intended design, this record is meant to represent exactly the three resources that were provisioned for this specific workload, bound together as one identity: a storage binding, a network binding, and an execution profile.

Storage and network provisioning both work as intended end to end. The Volume Controller and Network Controller each issue a binding identifier when they provision a resource, and later, before a workload is admitted onto the fabric, those identifiers are checked against the issuing controller to confirm the binding still exists and is still valid. Anything unregistered, expired, or otherwise invalid is correctly rejected at that stage.

The execution profile side of the pipeline does not carry the same guarantee. When the composition service assembles the final runtime record, it does not simply pass through the execution profile it was handed by the Execution Profile Controller. Instead, it re-derives an execution profile at assembly time using only the sandbox policy name as a lookup key, and takes whichever profile that lookup happens to return. Because more than one execution profile can legitimately share the same sandbox policy name while differing in the tool allowlist and CPU policy attached to them, this re-derivation step can quietly substitute a different concrete profile than the one that was actually issued for this workload — while the sandbox policy name itself still looks correct.

Downstream, the component responsible for admitting a workload onto the compute fabric checks that the storage and network identities in the composed record are still registered and valid with their respective controllers. It performs no equivalent check for the execution profile identity in the record, since the composition service is treated as the sole authority for assembling that field. The final stage, which actually configures and launches the container, takes the composed record at face value and applies whatever sandbox profile, tool allowlist, and CPU policy are present in it, without re-deriving or re-checking any of the three identities itself.

Taken together, a workload can be scheduled and launched successfully — with genuinely valid storage and genuinely valid networking — while running under a different tool allowlist and CPU policy than the one that was actually provisioned for it, and no component anywhere in the pipeline ever observes an inconsistency.


