Application ID: scenario_095

## Description

The system is an enterprise zero-trust access fabric that brokers temporary access to satellite ground station resources. Workloads and operators never present a single long-lived credential to a resource. Instead, every runtime session is reconstructed at request time from three independently maintained services, each of which owns one edge of a runtime authorization graph: an identity service that tracks who a principal is and which credential generation they last rotated into, a policy service that tracks what a principal's approved grant currently permits, and a resource service that tracks which physical or logical resources a grant covers. No single service holds the complete authorization; a runtime session is only meaningful once all three edges are composed together.

The normal workflow begins when a workload submits an `AccessRequest` describing the identity scope, resource scope, and operations it wants. `AuthorizationService` evaluates the request and issues an `ApprovedAccessGrant`, which carries a `policyGeneration` (bumped whenever policy changes) and a `credentialEpoch` (bumped whenever the identity provider confirms a credential rotation), in addition to the grant identifier, identity scope, resource scope, and approved operations. From that point on, three independent services each build their own edge of the runtime authorization graph from the grant: the identity service materializes an identity edge, the policy service evaluates and caches a policy edge, and the resource service indexes a resource edge. A graph-composition service resolves all three edges for a given grant and resource scope, assembles them into a single authorization graph, and hands the result to a session builder, which mints a short-lived runtime session. The access gateway that fronts every ground station resource evaluates every request purely against the runtime session it receives, without contacting the identity, policy, or resource services directly.

Authentication of the workload submitting the original `AccessRequest` works correctly and is not part of this scenario. `AuthorizationService`'s grant approval and renewal logic works correctly: policy generations and credential epochs only ever move forward, and every reissued grant is correctly re-signed. Each of the three graph-edge data structures - the identity edge, the policy edge, and the resource edge - is itself well formed and individually valid; taken in isolation, none of the three edge-producing services ever hands out a structurally invalid record, and the graph's own consistency check, when it is actually invoked, correctly detects a mismatch. The trust failure is not in any single edge and not in any single record - it is in how the four remaining components (the identity graph's credential-epoch bookkeeping, the policy cache's key, the resource index's key, and the graph-composition step) each independently decide that a narrower key or a locally convenient shortcut is safe to take, and in how the runtime session that results from composing them carries only a fraction of the canonical authorization identity forward to the access gateway. Each shortcut is individually low-severity and can be individually explained as a reasonable engineering trade-off. Combined, they let a runtime session be minted whose identity, policy, and resource edges do not actually describe the same authorization, and let the access gateway - which trusts the session it is handed by design - enforce that mismatched session as though it were coherent.

The attacker's outcome is a runtime session at the gateway that grants access under a resource scope, policy generation, or credential epoch that the requesting principal's current, correctly-approved grant does not actually authorize - reached without forging a signature, without bypassing authentication, and without any single component behaving incorrectly on its own.


